  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cooler day on Friday with plenty of sun and \ndiminishing wind.\n\nFriday will continue the stretch of beautiful Fall weather for the \nregion as we say goodbye to any influence from Subtropical Storm \nJosephine, replaced by building high pressure encroaching from the \nnorth. The weakening gradient will mean less of that northerly wind \nthrough the day. After winds initially start out breezy, gusting 15-\n25 mph they'll end the day much lighter. Overnight in fact, under \nhigh pressure winds will become calm under clear skies, so lows were \ntrended toward the colder MOS guidance, in the upper 20s to low 30s. \nThis, after a cooler day on Friday after cold advection kicks in \naloft; 925mb temps drop from 9C today to 0-1C on Friday leading to \nhighs in the 50s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Prolonged stretch of dry weather with \nseasonable temperatures prevails this weekend into much of next \nweek.\n\nA prolonged stretch of dry but pleasant autumn weather is in store \nfor Southern New England spanning this weekend then continuing into \na large portion of the upcoming workweek. Expansive area of surface \nhigh pressure of around 1030-1034 mb will remain anchored across the \nNortheast and mid-Atlantic region, with mild days and cool nights \nwith strong diurnal temperature ranges. About the only nuanced \ndifference is that a cold front sags southward Monday night into \nTuesday with a cooler northern-stream airmass filtering southward; \nbut the front is moisture-starved and only seems capable of \nproducing some enhanced cloudiness Monday evening and a cooldown in \nhigh temps moving into next week. Mildest days look to be this \nweekend with temperatures in the mid to upper 60s, but there could \nbe a shot at 70 degrees with several days of dry weather serving as \na mild feedback mechanism for overachieving maximum temps over \nguidance. Highs then cool off into the mid 50s to low 60s for early \nnext week as the cooler lower-level airmass works it way in (925 mb \ntemps dip to around +2 to +4C), only modifying toward mid to late \nnext week.",
